Draw the three boundary lines \(x + 2y = 10\), \(x + y = 6\) and \(3x + y = 8\) in the first quadrant. Every constraint is of \(\ge\) type, so the feasible region is the part of the first quadrant lying on the far side of all three lines from the origin; it is unbounded above and to the right.

Its corner points are:

\(A(0, 8)\), where \(3x + y = 8\) meets the y-axis;

\(B(1, 5)\), where \(3x + y = 8\) meets \(x + y = 6\);

\(C(2, 4)\), where \(x + y = 6\) meets \(x + 2y = 10\);

\(D(10, 0)\), where \(x + 2y = 10\) meets the x-axis.

Evaluating \(Z = 3x + 5y\) at each: \(Z(A) = 40\), \(Z(B) = 3 + 25 = 28\), \(Z(C) = 6 + 20 = 26\), \(Z(D) = 30\).

The smallest value is \(26\). Because the region is unbounded, this needs the usual confirmation: the open half-plane \(3x + 5y < 26\) has no point in common with the feasible region, so the minimum really is attained. Hence \(Z\) is minimum at \((2, 4)\), with \(Z_{\min} = 26\).
